{
  "summary": "The round 7 proposal is the most concrete and implementable design yet, but it has several underexamined weaknesses. First, the 'extractive questions' approach — asking the LLM to list new points, contradicted points, and agreed points — is presented as more objective than verdict-based judgment, but enumeration is itself a judgment call. The LLM decides what constitutes a 'point,' how to delineate one point from another (granularity manipulation can game any ratio), and whether two points across cycles are 'substantially the same.' A model could inflate new_points by splitting a single idea into sub-points, or deflate it by lumping distinct concerns together. The ratios (0.15 for stall, 0.7 for consensus) inherit the same 'magic number' critique leveled at the delta_score thresholds in round 4 — there is no empirical or theoretical basis for these cutoffs, and they will behave unpredictably across different topic complexities and agent verbosity levels. Second, the progressive threshold schedule (25% -> 35% -> 50% novelty required) assumes that ideation should become more productive over time to justify continuation, but this inverts the natural dynamics of refinement — later rounds typically produce fewer but higher-quality refinements, not more. A legitimate deep-dive into implementation tradeoffs at round 8 might surface only one or two critical insights that are worth more than ten surface-level points from round 2. The progressive threshold penalizes exactly the kind of focused, late-stage convergence the system is trying to detect. Third, the checkpoint schedule (rounds 2, 4, 8, then every 4) front-loads evaluation but creates an odd gap — no checkpoint between rounds 4 and 8 means four rounds run unchecked, which contradicts the 'fail fast' philosophy the proposal claims to embody. Fourth, the proposal adds significant implementation complexity (new item schema fields, checkpoint loop in run_ideating, augmented synthesis prompts, checkpoint history metadata) for a feature whose core value proposition — saving rounds by stopping early — is only meaningful when the hard cap is high enough that early termination saves substantial cost. With a 12-round default, you get checkpoints at rounds 2, 4, 8, and 12 — at most 3 opportunities to stop early, saving at most 10 rounds. The implementation cost may not justify the savings, especially when a simpler alternative exists: just run a fixed number of rounds (say 4-8) and let the user configure it per-board, with no convergence detection at all. The current ideation system already works with fixed rounds. The real question this discussion keeps circling is whether automated convergence detection adds enough value over a simple configurable round count to justify its complexity, unreliable heuristics, and additional API costs — and after 7 rounds of debate, no proposal has convincingly demonstrated that it does."
